  "retries": 0,
  "findings": {
    "camera_fault_depends_on_the_shutter": "capture.check_state() returns early unless the capture window is open, so disabling the camera during SHIFT or ROTATE_180 is not a mission fault. Both probes ran to ACQUIRED with 12 archived blocks: the next pass re-requests the sensor and recovers it transparently. The probe asserted only the invariants -- nothing fires at injection, a defined terminal state is reached, any fault is CAMERA_* and stops the vehicle -- rather than guessing which way it goes.",
    "reaction_paths_differ_by_an_order_of_magnitude": "the camera fault travels on the sensor heartbeat and fired in 15-35 ms; the localization fault waits out wall_timeout_s and fired in 320-360 ms. Both are within budget, but they are not the same mechanism and should not be quoted as one reaction time.",
    "rotation_needs_a_yaw_criterion": "fault_loc_rotate stopped within 0.0014 m against a 0.1015 m budget, which an in-place rotation satisfies by standing still. The yaw evidence is what carries it: 0.0654 rad/s at injection, 0.0353 rad swept after the fault, settling to zero.",
    "discarded_tails_are_a_correct_empty_archive": "three probes archived no block because the closing tail fell under the 1000-row floor; the sensor recorded tail_discarded and the probe checks for that rather than demanding a block."
  },
  "reaction_budget_s": 0.65,
  "stop_thresholds": {
    "stopped_speed_m_s": 0.025,
    "stopped_yaw_rate_rad_s": 0.015
  },
  "not_covered": [
    "camera_disabled was not injected in the runout phase: the shutter closes there only once the camera passes the planned over-run, so the outcome depends on which side of that point the injection lands and would not be reproducible",
    "one region size, one noise profile, one injection per phase; not a repeat trial",
    "approach phase not injected: it carries no capture and duplicates the shift result"
  ]
